.\" strings.1 -- maux's strings(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text), so it carries no
.\" third-party notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as
.\" od.1. Rendered at build time to build/man/STRINGS.TXT and
.\" staged on the play image as /usr/man/STRINGS:
.\" `more /usr/man/STRINGS`.
.TH STRINGS 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
strings \- find printable strings in a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B strings
[
.B \-a
] [
.BI \-n " number"
] [
.BI \-t " format"
] [
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Strings
prints every run of
.I number
(default 4, capped at 255 in this implementation) or more
printable bytes in
.I file
(standard input if none is given), each terminated by a
non\-printable byte or end of file \(em the terminator itself
is never printed.  With more than one
.I file
operand, each is scanned in turn with no header between them,
matching this project's BSD reference.
.PP
A byte is printable if it falls in
.B $20\-$7E
(plain ASCII) OR
.B $A0\-$FE
(this tree's own high\-bit text convention) \(em a raw file has
no liba string convention of its own, so both count.  A
qualifying byte is written to standard output with its high bit
set, this tree's own text convention, regardless of which half
of that rule it qualified under.
.PP
.B \-a
is accepted and does nothing: this implementation has no
object\-file section table to restrict to, so the whole file is
always scanned.
.PP
.BI \-t " format"
prefixes each string with its byte offset from the start of
.I file
(resetting to 0 per file), as a bare number with no leading
zeros:
.B d
decimal,
.B o
octal,
.B x
lower\-case hexadecimal.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/STRINGS  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
od(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 1 if any file operand could not be opened
(the rest are still scanned); 2 for a usage error (an
unrecognised option, a non\-numeric or out\-of\-range
.BR \-n ,
or a
.B \-t
argument other than a single
.BR d , " o" ,
or
.BR x ).
.SH BUGS
.BI \-n " number"
is capped at 255 (see above) \(em a longer minimum is refused
as a usage error rather than honoured.
